  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Result — the rule holds for `[3,2,0]` too\n\n`W1` (excitations on the first GT pattern only) is 15-dimensional and contains `<0|`. `B` restricted\nto it is diagonalisable with simple spectrum, and all 15 eigenvectors were confirmed to be left\neigenvectors of `B(u)` at three `u` values never used to build them. (As with `[3,1,0]`, a single\n`B(7/3)` gives only **13** distinct eigenvalues — accidental collisions — so a generic combination of\n`B` at several `u` is required.)\n\n**Exactly 6 of the 15 localize, each on its own, and they are precisely `m11 = m22`:**\n\n| localizes | `(m12; m11, m22)` |\n|---|---|\n| **yes** (6) | `(2;0,0) (2;1,1) (2;2,2) (3;0,0) (3;1,1) (3;2,2)` |\n| no (9) | `(2;1,0) (2;2,0) (2;2,1) (3;1,0) (3;2,0) (3;2,1) (3;3,0) (3;3,1) (3;3,2)` |\n\nSite-1 slots: `{4, 7, 10, 12, 14, 15}` — identical to the slots predicted by `m11 == m22`, and the\npredicate `localizes <=> m11 == m22` is `True` on all 15 states.\n\nCount: `(l1-l2+1)(l2-l3+1) = 2 x 3 = 6`.\n\n### Why this case is a real test\n\n`[3,2,0]` is the *mirror* of `[3,1,0]`: there `l1-l2 = 2, l2-l3 = 1` (so `m12` had 3 values and\n`m22` 2), here `l1-l2 = 1, l2-l3 = 2` (`m12` has 2 values, `m22` 3). The rule is not symmetric in\n`m12` and `m22` — it constrains `m11, m22` and leaves `m12` free — so the two reps give genuinely\ndifferent patterns, and both come out right.\n\n### Status of the rule\n\n| `lambda` | dim | measured | localizing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `[2,1,0]` (x) `[2,1,0]` | 8 | yes | 4, slots `{3,5,7,8}` |\n| `[2,1,0]` (x) `[1,0,0]` | 8 | yes | 4, slots `{3,5,7,8}` |\n| `[3,1,0]` (x) `[3,1,0]` | 15 | yes | 6, slots `{7,9,11,13,14,15}` |\n| `[3,2,0]` (x) `[3,2,0]` | 15 | yes | 6, slots `{4,7,10,12,14,15}` |\n\nStill unmeasured: any `lambda` with `l2 = l3` (e.g. `[3,0,0]`, `[2,2,0]`), where the ground site-2\nroot triple has a repeated entry and the null-space intersection needs an extra projection step\nbefore it isolates the ground block.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
